WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.380
Why does a cell pay to attach CO₂, only to throw it away two steps later?

2
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:11.020
That apparent waste is the trick that makes fatty acid synthesis move forward.

3
00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:19.517
In its simplest form, begin with acetyl CoA already in the cytoplasm.

4
00:00:20.877 --> 00:00:22.317
Its two carbons are the primer.

5
00:00:23.717 --> 00:00:29.357
Every later pair enters through malonyl CoA, the extender made by acetyl CoA

6
00:00:29.357 --> 00:00:31.097
carboxylase, or ACC.

7
00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:37.547
ACC combines acetyl CoA with bicarbonate and spends one ATP.

8
00:00:39.127 --> 00:00:43.607
The product is malonyl CoA, carrying an extra carboxyl group.

9
00:00:44.907 --> 00:00:50.247
Because the malonyl CoA made for synthesis has essentially one fate, this is the

10
00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:51.247
committed step.

11
00:00:51.967 --> 00:00:59.307
Now predict. After fatty acid synthase uses malonyl CoA, where will that newly

12
00:00:59.307 --> 00:01:01.507
attached carboxyl carbon end up?

13
00:01:02.487 --> 00:01:04.027
In the chain, or somewhere else?

14
00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:13.110
Fatty acid synthase joins the extender to the primer.

15
00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:19.130
During condensation, the extra carboxyl group leaves as CO₂.

16
00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:24.990
So little to none of a labeled bicarbonate carbon remains in palmitate.

17
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.460
That loss is useful.

18
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.360
Joining two acetyl units directly is uphill.

19
00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.120
Releasing CO₂ is strongly downhill, so coupling the events pulls the new

20
00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.620
carbon–carbon bond forward.

21
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.060
The cell spent ATP to load this molecular spring.

22
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:48.893
Condensation leaves a carbonyl that the chain cannot keep.

23
00:01:50.313 --> 00:01:55.593
Fatty acid synthase then finishes CRDR: reduction, dehydration, reduction.

24
00:01:57.353 --> 00:02:03.533
Two NADPH supply the four electrons needed to turn that carbonyl carbon into a

25
00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:04.533
saturated segment.

26
00:02:05.617 --> 00:02:12.697
Here is the rule. After the primer, each CRDR turn adds two carbons as one malonyl

27
00:02:12.697 --> 00:02:20.717
CoA. That addition costs one ATP at ACC and two NADPH at fatty acid synthase.

28
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:24.760
The four-carbon chain returns for another turn.

29
00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:31.700
The same machine repeats until seven malonyl units have added fourteen carbons to

30
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:32.700
the two-carbon primer.

31
00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:39.900
Fatty acid synthase then releases palmitate, a saturated 16-carbon fatty acid.

32
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:44.360
In mammals, fatty acid synthase mostly stops there.

33
00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.540
Longer chains and double bonds are handled later.

34
00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.100
So this film’s rule covers the saturated chain built to palmitate.

35
00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.980
It starts from acetyl CoA already in the cytoplasm.

36
00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:03.210
So the discarded carbon was never a building block.

37
00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:09.750
It was a temporary handle, bought with ATP, whose release drives each condensation.

38
00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:15.610
Count the malonyl additions, and you count both the two-carbon gains and their cost.
